  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the line $2x-y-4=0$ passes through the points $R(0,-4)$ and $S(1,-2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $R$ and $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${R}^{\u2032}(8,-12)$ and ${S}^{\u2032}(3,-6).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${R}^{\u2032}$ and ${S}^{\u2032}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-12) & =\\frac{-6-(-12)}{3-8}(x-8) \\\\ y+12 & =-\\frac{6}{5}(x-8) \\\\ 5y+60 & =-6x+48 \\\\ 6x+5y+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
